    excerpt: "AI startup funding is breaking records, but the nature of what's getting funded has shifted. Here's what investors are actually looking for in 2026.",
    content: `<h2>The Numbers</h2><p>Eleven billion dollars. That's how much was invested in AI startups this quarter. It's a record. But the story behind the numbers is more interesting than the numbers themselves.</p><h2>What Changed</h2><p>Last year, investors were throwing money at anything with "AI" in the pitch deck. This year? They're actually asking questions. Due diligence is back.</p><p>The winners aren't the "AI for X" companies anymore. They're the "AI actually solves Y problem" companies. Investors have learned that wrapping a chat interface around an API doesn't make a business.</p><h2>What's Getting Funded</h2><p><strong>AI agents.</strong> Autonomous systems that can execute real work. Not chatbots. Not assistants. Agents that can do jobs.</p><p><strong>Vertical AI.</strong> AI designed for specific industries: healthcare, legal, manufacturing. Horizontal tools are out; vertical solutions are in.</p><p><strong>AI infrastructure.</strong> The picks and shovels play. Tools that help other AI companies build faster.</p><h2>What's NOT Getting Funded</h2><p><strong>Generic LLMs.</strong> Unless you're Google, Microsoft, or Anthropic, don't try to build another foundational model. The market has consolidated.</p><p><strong>AI wrappers.</strong> Companies that just put a UI on top of someone else's API. Investors have seen thousands of these fail.</p><p><strong>Consumer AI apps.</strong> Hard to build, hard to monetize, easy to copy. Unless you have a viral distribution strategy, skip consumer.</p><h2>The Implication for Creators</h2><p>If you're building an AI product, the message is clear: solve a specific problem for a specific customer. Don't try to be everything to everyone.</p><p>For the rest of us, this means better tools are coming. The companies getting funded are building real solutions, not demos.</p><p><strong>Rating: 7/10</strong> — Healthy correction in the market. The bubble isn't bursting — it's maturing.</p>`,
